<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cortes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> were the representative assemblies of the Iberian kingdoms, in which the king met the clergy, nobility and towns to grant taxes, hear grievances and swear to uphold the laws. That of León in 1188 is among the earliest European parliaments to include town representatives; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Aragon, Catalonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and Portugal had their own. The name passed to the modern parliaments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal.</p></div>
